{"author":{"id":"1d5f412cdeafe9987ad9876b0995bf5032718543d34c038a50cdb41808e9874c"},"ops":[{"type":5,"timestamp":1788305121,"nonce":"7m5Ow8dP6nHJ1PSYPB9sebe4HsU=","metadata":{"github-id":"LE_lADOULVc1M8AAAABPPhcdc8AAAAHEtKmOA"},"added":["bug"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"timestamp":1788305121,"nonce":"G5fZX/145z/qfP0aKNovFIsm524=","metadata":{"github-id":"LE_lADOULVc1M8AAAABPPhcdc8AAAAHEtKnAw"},"added":["data-integrity"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"timestamp":1788305121,"nonce":"H0L0KARTSpCkP2Wh8wxNPqMTiiw=","metadata":{"github-id":"LE_lADOULVc1M8AAAABPPhcdc8AAAAHEtKnhQ"},"added":["compat"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"timestamp":1788306369,"nonce":"0W7tHL+QYRIwmkOwlSwz0f0sSqE=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOULVc1M8AAAABR_QabQ","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/torram-ng/issues/3#issuecomment-5502147181"},"message":"Two clarifications after testing this against real data.\n\n**The upstream README explains this as a format limitation. It is not one.** The note reads:\n\n\u003e Due to technical implementation of how files are being split into chunks in .torrent, sometimes it's impossible to check md5 sum of first and last chunk of the file.\n\nA spanning piece is perfectly verifiable — it just has to be read from every file it covers, which is what every BitTorrent client does. Demonstrated on the generated multi-file fixture, piece 3:\n\n```\npiece 3 expected sha1: 577753a25b1f5c00839a7649344fa00359784a3d\n\n  reading piece_length from a.bin alone (what this code does):\n    got 1696 of 32768 bytes  -\u003e MISMATCH\n\n  reading across the boundary:\n    1696 bytes from a.bin\n       0 bytes from empty.dat\n      10 bytes from tiny.txt\n   31062 bytes from subdir/b.bin\n    = 32768 bytes         -\u003e MATCH\n```\n\nSo the observed `[3 of 4]` reporting is expected behaviour for this implementation, but the underlying cause is the per-file architecture, not the torrent format. It is fixable. (The hashes are SHA-1, not md5, which the README also gets wrong.)\n\n**Scale of the knock-on effect.** A file that fits entirely inside a piece starting in an earlier file gets no piece attributed to it at all, is reported `[0 of 0] (Bad)`, and `suggest_method` returns `S`. On `BigBuckBunny_124_archive.torrent`:\n\n```\nfiles receiving zero pieces: 7 of 12\n     590 B  Description.txt\n     669 B  LegalTorrents.txt\n      86 B  License.txt\n    8416 B  __ia_thumb.jpg\n   51283 B  blender_foundation_-_big_buck_bunny_720p.torrent\n     194 B  ..._torrent.tlg\n     195 B  ..._torrent.txt\n```\n\nWorth being precise about the consequence: interactively the Skip is only a *default* and can be overridden by choosing a candidate number, so nothing is lost if someone is watching. Under `-s`/`-ss` there is no prompt and those files are dropped from the output with the run still exiting 0.","files":null}]}